---

A share is a folder on one device, under a name. The device that shares it
keeps the files where they are; the rest of the swarm sees the share's listing
and fetches file contents on request.

# [Sharing a folder]($heading.id('sharing'))

From the command line, add `share = "Name:/path"` to the configuration
(repeatable) or pass `--share Name:/path` when starting the node.

In the app, open **Shared folders** and press **Choose folder…**. The system's
own folder picker opens (GTK on Linux, the Storage Access Framework on
Android); the picked folder shows with an editable name, then **Share**. Shares
are saved in the app's configuration and come back after a restart. On Android,
folders in shared storage (the camera roll, Pictures, Documents, Download) can
be shared; folders from cloud providers have no local path and are refused.

# [What gets copied, and what does not]($heading.id('what-moves'))

Sharing does not copy your files into NomadFS. A background scan turns the
folder into a Merkle tree:

- Files are split into content-defined chunks (FastCDC); each chunk is a block
  addressed by its SHA-256 hash.
- For file contents, NomadFS records only where each block is (file, offset,
  length) and serves it from the file itself, hashing it again on every read.
  Only the directory blocks and the blocks that index a file's chunks are
  stored.
- The scan reuses unchanged files by size and modification time, and runs again
  every 30 seconds, so the share follows the folder. What it learned is kept in
  `scan-cache` in the storage folder, so a restart doesn't read every file
  again.

A large folder takes a while to scan the first time (every file is read and
hashed once). It shows up on your other devices within seconds anyway and fills
in as the scan goes: what has been scanned so far can already be browsed and
opened. The app shows it as indexing until the first scan finishes.

# [Browsing another device]($heading.id('browsing'))

Each device answers the others with its list of shares (their root blocks and
the device's name). Browsing a share loads one directory block at a time, so
opening a folder costs one small request whatever the size of the files in it.

In the app, **Browse** lists every reachable device; pick one to walk its
shares with breadcrumbs. On wide windows the reachable devices are also listed
in the drawer.

# [Opening and downloading files]($heading.id('opening'))

Reading a file asks only for the blocks that cover the bytes being read. Every
block that arrives is checked against its hash before it is kept, and kept
blocks are kept in a read cache, so a file opened once opens again without the
other device. The cache holds 2 GB by default (`cache_mb` under `[storage]`);
when it is full, the blocks read longest ago make room. **About → Read cache**
in the app, or `nomadfs --cache`, shows its size; **Clear cache**, or
`nomadfs --clear-cache`, empties it. Files you wrote yourself are never part of
the cache.

The app's viewer shows images, video, audio, PDF and text, with previous and
next through the folder (arrow keys and Esc on the desktop). It reads the file
in 4 MiB pieces, so it shows progress and works the same on Android.
**Download** saves a copy in `~/Downloads/NomadFS` (the shared `Download/NomadFS` folder on Android); a name that exists already gets a number.

# [The Linux mount]($heading.id('mount'))

On Linux everything can be mounted as an ordinary folder that any program can
open:

```
<mount>/
├── Laptop/
│   └── Documents/…
├── Xiaomi M2007J20CG/
│   └── Camera/…
└── Swarm/…
```

- `<mount>/<device>/<share>/…` is read-only (files `0444`, folders `0555`).
- `<mount>/Swarm` is the [swarm folder]($link.page('docs/swarm-folder')) and is
  writable.

From the command line:

```sh
nomadfs --mount ~/NomadFS
```

It runs the node, mounts, and unmounts on Ctrl-C or `SIGTERM`. In the desktop
app, the **Mount** tab mounts at `~/NomadFS` by default (**Change** picks
another folder), and **Open folder** opens it in your file manager. The tray
icon has the same switch, so the mount keeps working with the window closed;
**Quit** in the tray unmounts before exiting. Mounting over a stale NomadFS
mount at the same folder clears it first.

The driver speaks the kernel's FUSE protocol on `/dev/fuse` directly: there is
no libfuse or libc dependency. `fusermount3` performs the unprivileged mount, so
install `fuse3` if it is missing. Mounting is available on Linux only; the
**Mount** tab does not appear elsewhere.
